 "authorLine": "Agent run s5, 2026-10-30",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: about three thousand documents in the 551 first-edition volumes of the index use the vocabulary of corruption, bribery or graft; on a forty-document test, eight in forty tie it to an American choice. The choices are: supervising rather than dropping a corrupt client (China 1944 to 1949, Greece 1947, the Philippines 1985); making aid or a loan depend on reform (Cuba 1922, the Philippines 1950 and 1952), with Burma refusing American money for the same reason in 1955; declining to press an ally hard enough to change it (Vietnam 1960 to 1972); and, from 1975, regulating and negotiating over the bribery done by American firms. Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came mostly from the Central Files of the Department of State, Record Group 59 (23 of 37 with a source line), with one from the Conference Files lot (Record Group 59, entry A1 3051B, identifier 602875, 1949 to 1972, unrestricted; probable match by name), two from the Policy Planning Staff lot 89 D 149, seven from presidential libraries and three from non-National Archives holdings; the editors' unprinted citations over the hit documents point to the Carter, Johnson and Nixon libraries and to the Executive Secretariat NSC Policy Papers series (Record Group 59, entry A1 1586E, identifier 2839192, 1947 to 1979, partly restricted). The two channels are not summed. What this request cannot get from FRUS: a rate (FRUS is a selection); whether the stated reason was the real one (foreign archives); years the index barely holds (before 1860, and the 1990s); the companies' own records of the 1970s payments (the Senate subcommittee and Securities and Exchange Commission files); and intelligence reporting on foreign corruption.",
